    "rationale": "The primary mechanism is retained retry state across pooled request lifecycles. Baseline O1 couples high p95 latency (412 ms) with elevated retries (3.1/request). O2 shows that halving concurrency reduces p95 to 286 ms, but that load-relief correlation is non-discriminating and does not identify queue saturation. O3 further weakens queue saturation because CPU stays below 48% and queue occupancy below 62%. The first causal intervention, E_force_retry_reset, leaves pool reuse enabled but resets retry state at each request boundary; it reduces p95 to 219 ms and mean retries to 1.0, directly supporting H_retry_state. The second causal intervention, E_queue_4x, increases queue capacity fourfold while holding concurrency and retry policy fixed; p95 remains 405 ms and retries 3.0, directly rejecting H_queue as the primary cause. H_clock_bucket is also rejected as the primary mechanism because the retry-reset intervention fixes the regression without changing timeout timestamp quantization. Thus the concurrency effect in O2 is load relief, not the causal mechanism."
